# Coverage floor. May only RISE; the gate's `fix` half refuses to record a
# drop (SPEC.md V27). Raised from 0.0 at the scaffold to 99.17.
#
# EVERY LIBRARY SOURCE LINE IS EXECUTED. `cargo llvm-cov --show-missing-lines`
# reports uncovered lines in exactly one file: `src/main.rs`. The per-file
# text reports show zero uncovered lines in classify.rs, cli.rs, config.rs,
# corpus.rs, scan.rs and statement.rs. The small residue the merged summary
# still attributes to cli.rs and scan.rs is lib-vs-bin instantiation
# accounting, not source anyone can reach.
#
# The remaining 11 lines are `fn main`: reading argv, the working directory
# and HOME, then calling into `cli`. A binary entry point cannot be called
# by a unit test, so those lines are unverifiable rather than unverified.
#
# THEY ARE DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED from the measurement. Passing
# `--ignore-filename-regex 'src/main\.rs'` would report 99.78 and would
# also remove the only pressure keeping main.rs thin -- code moved there
# would stop counting against this number, which is precisely the incentive
# that put 37 lines in it before. Leaving the gap visible is what keeps the
# binary an edge instead of a hiding place.
#
# Three earlier rounds were REFACTORS rather than new tests, and each was
# the right fix on its own terms:
#   * dispatch moved out of main.rs -- a binary's logic is untestable
#   * `perform` returns u8, not ExitCode -- ExitCode has no comparison, so
#     tests could call every arm and assert nothing
#   * `Matcher` replaced `GlobSet` -- `GlobSetBuilder::build()` is fallible
#     but cannot fail after per-glob validation, so three lines of error
#     handling existed that no test could reach. Removing the unreachable
#     branch beat explaining it.
#
# The floor RISES WITH THE CODE, and the gate enforces that rather than
# asking politely (V27). A rise of more than 0.5 above this number FAILS
# the coverage step; `hk fix --all` records it and clears the failure.
#
# That enforcement was missing until it was tested. hk runs a step's `fix`
# only when its `check` FAILS, so a rise -- which passed -- never reached
# the code that records it, and the message printed on a rise named a
# command that does not exist. The floor would have drifted further below
# reality with every well-tested commit, protecting less and less while
# reporting green.
#
# Do not raise this by excluding files.

lines 99.26
